in this episode of cloud performance  atlas I helped some VR expert with their  disk performance does this VR headset  make my performance look awesome stay  tuned to find out VR ignition is a  company specializing in the processing  of content for VR and AR applications  their product can scale to handle  massive point clouds across thousands of  CPUs using the Google cloud platform and  due to the nature of their algorithms  had a large need for local SSDs to  handle their out of court algorithms  however they were confused because they  weren't getting the type of read  performance they were expecting from  these high-performance  discs I'll spare you some of the  debugging details but it turned out that  the problem was with their disk  interface see for local SSDs there's two  primary interfaces scuzzy and nvm II  scuzzy of course we've all seen for  years now and is the default for most  disk interfaces in our data centers and  via me however is the new kid on the  block designed with modern technologies  in mind to provide higher performance  now for example whereas scuzzy has one  cue for commands nvme is designed to  have up to 64,000 cues on moreover each  of those cues can in turn have up to  64,000 commands simultaneously now you  can switch what interface your machine  uses at creation time by passing along  the interface tag to the local SSD per  am and to show the performance  difference we can see the two  head-to-head we clearly see that nvme  has an advantage with respect to read  performance and a slight advantage over  write performance but here's the catch  in order to use nvm II you have to have  a disk image that supports it and this  was the thing that was hurting VR  ignition firstly they weren't setting  the flag and secondly they weren't using  a custom image for their builds that  didn't have nvme support thankfully  after a decent amount of technical  discussion they were able to move to a  public image that was already optimized  for nvme which let them get the big  boost in performance if you'd like to  know more about this topic check out the  article linked in the description below  and don't forget to check out the rest  of the cloud performance Atlas content  because when it comes to performance  every millisecond counts  you
 

